\section{A Foreign Declaration Is More Than an Arrow}

A type-safe foreign interface must account for at least three agreements: what the arguments mean, how their bits cross the ABI, and who may access their storage for how long. Two declarations can agree about the first and disagree on register classes for a struct result, signed extension of a narrow value, or the thread on which a callback may run. A correct stub is a typed adapter across all three agreements, not a cast from a symbol address to the desired function type.

Michael Furr and Jeffrey S.\ Foster's \emph{Checking Type Safety of Foreign Function Calls} studies two interfaces with different visibility into managed runtimes. Their O-Saffire analysis for OCaml and C uses representational types plus flow-sensitive facts for offsets and data tags, and effects to identify calls that may trigger collection. Their J-Saffire analysis for Java and JNI uses opaque object references and singleton types for descriptor strings, with polymorphism to analyze wrappers whose types depend on the descriptors. The two analyses illustrate why an FFI checker should model the operations foreign code really uses rather than merely duplicate the host type checker on a second syntax tree. The paper's soundness claims are restricted and do not cover arbitrary C operations.

\section{Worked Stub: Carry a Root, Not an Interior Address}

Suppose a moving collector provides a local root slot containing a managed byte array. A trusted stub calls a managed helper that may allocate, then invokes a synchronous C routine to compute a checksum. C expects a native byte pointer and a length. It borrows the bytes only until the call returns. A safe copying adapter follows this sequence:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Register the managed array in a root slot before the helper call. Do not keep a raw pointer to its payload across the call.
  \item After the helper returns, read the array through the root slot; a collection may have updated the slot. Check that the byte count is representable in the C length type, and copy into a native buffer. The acquisition and copy must use a runtime-supported operation or a region in which movement of the source is ruled out.
  \item Call C on the native buffer with the checked length, translate the return status and checksum, then release the buffer and root on every exit path.
\end{enumerate}
The root keeps the object live and supplies a current reference after movement; it does not generally stabilize a previously extracted interior address. A pinning adapter can instead hold a runtime-supported pin until return, but needs the same no-retention contract and may constrain the collector. Marcus Crestani's \emph{Foreign-Function Interfaces for Garbage-Collected Programming Languages} surveys such design choices in the context of Scheme implementations and explains why bookkeeping for collector cooperation influences portability and usability. If the native routine retains the pointer or schedules a callback on another thread, this local adapter is insufficient: a separate ownership transfer or retained-handle protocol is needed.

\section{What the Boundary Verifier Can Establish}

A practical compiler can generate stubs from declarations recording layout and calling convention, ownership of pointer arguments and results, allocation effects, and callback policy. Separate an effect like ``may enter managed code'' from ordinary C allocation: even a function with no explicit allocation of managed objects can call back and cause collection. Propagating that effect through wrappers lets a verifier check root registration at each relevant call. The dataflow invariant is that every managed reference live across a collection point is discoverable by the runtime as required, and any surviving native address is either independently allocated or known stable for its entire use.

Neither a precise declaration nor a verified stub can make an arbitrary native body honor a borrow or return a valid value. Check cheap interface properties where possible, document which promises are trusted, and use isolation when foreign code itself cannot be trusted. Test ABI layout on each supported target, force collection at callback and helper boundaries, and exercise exceptional cleanup. Chapter~\ref{ch:calling-conventions} explains the ABI substrate; Markdown Dossier Chapter~42 explores versioned ABI negotiation, which can help match descriptors but does not by itself verify ownership or root discipline.
