\section{Comparing Native Layouts Before Generating a Stub}

An adapter can model the type correctly yet generate the wrong
field loads because the managed-side descriptor was built using
a different packing rule. For a concrete check, use a trusted C
probe compiled with the same headers, target, and layout options
as the native library. The probe reports record size, alignment,
and $(\mathit{offset},\mathit{width})$ for each named field of
a plain fixed-width record. Compare this with the compiler's
predicted descriptor before allowing direct marshalling. Assume
identical scalar representations, byte order, and field meanings.
The output is a layout match or the mismatching entries, not a
claim that the record may be passed by value with any calling
convention.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input predicted layout $P$, native probe layout $N$
  \Output layout verdict and mismatch list
  \State $D\gets$ empty list
  \ForAll{$k$ in $\{\mathrm{size},\mathrm{alignment}\}$}
    \State record a difference in $D$ if $P[k]\ne N[k]$
    \Comment{Tail padding matters for array stride}
  \EndFor
  \ForAll{$f$ in the union of their field names}
    \State $p\gets\Call{EntryOrMissing}{P,f}$
    \State $n\gets\Call{EntryOrMissing}{N,f}$
    \If{$p\ne n$}
      \State append $(f,p,n)$ to $D$
      \Comment{Equal size alone is not enough}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $(D=\varnothing,D)$
\end{sudocode}

Consider a target with one-byte alignment for \texttt{uint8\_t}
and four-byte alignment for \texttt{uint32\_t}. A descriptor
for an unpacked record with an eight-bit \texttt{kind} followed
by a 32-bit \texttt{count} predicts offsets $0$ and $4$, size
$8$, and alignment $4$. A library built under a one-byte
packing rule reports offsets $0$ and $1$, size $5$, and
alignment $1$. The checker reports three mismatches: size,
alignment, and \texttt{count} offset. On a little-endian
target the packed native bytes for \texttt{kind} $3$ and
\texttt{count} $258$ are \texttt{03 02 01 00 00}. Reading
four bytes starting at offset $4$ cannot recover that count
from this record. Reject direct layout access or use a C
accessor compiled for the native record.

\Cref{fig:visual-149} aligns the layouts by byte offset.
The predicted access starts at the last byte of the packed record,
not at the start of \texttt{count}.

